#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# polygon

#% text
# `polygon([[x1, y1], [x2, y2], ...])` is a graphics primitive: the filled polygon with these corners.

#% text
# `polygon([[p1, p2, ...], [q1, q2, ...], ...])` is several polygons.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The polygon is closed from its last corner back to its first.

#% item
# It is filled in the current colour, or as `face_form` says (`face_form()` leaves it empty); its edges are drawn only after an `edge_form` directive.

#% item
# Overlapping parts of a self-intersecting polygon are filled by the non-zero winding rule.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A triangle:

#% cell
graphics(polygon([[0, 0], [2, 0], [1, 1.5]]))
#% output
#= image("polygon.files/polygon-1.png")

#% text
# A yellow square with black edges:

#% cell
graphics([face_form(yellow), edge_form(black), polygon([[0, 0], [1, 0], [1, 1], [0, 1]])])
#% output
#= image("polygon.files/polygon-2.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A regular hexagon from computed corners:

#% cell
graphics([hue(0.55), polygon(table([cos(k*pi/3), sin(k*pi/3)], k, range(0, 5)))])
#% output
#= image("polygon.files/polygon-3.png")

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A corner must be a pair of coordinates:

#% cell
polygon([[0, 0], [1, 0], "corner"])
#% output
#! failure(argument, polygon, 1)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `rectangle`, `disk`, `line`, `face_form`, `edge_form`, `graphics`
